From 3bd8d79bcec153a06a031e3f8cbd6bcb1f9961eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 17:25:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] merge repairs: seven defect classes, and one the compiler
 could never have reported

The union merge auto-resolved most files, and "auto-resolved" hid seven places where BOTH parents had added the same thing. Six were compiler errors. The seventh was silent.

THE SILENT ONE, and it is why this commit matters. ggml.c and ggml-cpu.c each carried TWO designated-initializer rows for GGML_TYPE_TQ3_1S and GGML_TYPE_TQ4_1S in their type_traits tables. That is legal C: the LAST one wins, with no warning and no error. Ours sit inside #ifdef GGML_ARIFI_TURBO_WEIGHT_QUANTS; TurboQuant's are unconditional and came second, so TurboQuant's silently overrode ours AND defeated our build flag. No build would ever have surfaced it. Found by scanning the tables for repeated [GGML_TYPE_*] keys, not by compiling. The duplicates are functionally identical today so nothing was mis-decoded - but the same shape with different bodies is a wrong-kernel bug that ships green.

The six the compiler did catch:
- GGML_OP_COUNT: the merge added GGML_OP_TURBO_WHT on top of our 108, so both asserts are 109. COUNTED by compiling a probe that prints the enum, not by counting lines in the header - my line count said 108 and was wrong.
- ggml-common.h: block_tq3_1s / block_tq4_1s defined twice. Kept ours, deleted theirs.
- ops.cpp: 14 duplicate case GGML_TYPE_TQ*_1S labels across 7 switches. Deduped SWITCH-SCOPED, keeping the first per switch, with an assertion that no switch lost all handling for a value it previously had - a type that falls through to default aborts at runtime, which is the failure class this whole merge exists to remove. An earlier rule (drop every unguarded label) would have stripped two switches bare; it was reverted.
- llama-model-loader.cpp, llama-quant.cpp, ggml-quants.c: the same duplicate-label class.
- llama.h: LLAMA_FTYPE_MOSTLY_TQ3_1S / TQ4_1S declared twice at the same values.
- ggml-turbo-quant.c and ggml-arifi-turbo-weights.c both DEFINE the TQ weight quantizers, so ggml-base linked the same symbols twice. TurboQuant's TQ section is now fenced behind #ifndef GGML_ARIFI_TURBO_WEIGHT_QUANTS; its turbo2/3/4 KV codecs stay unconditional, which is asserted rather than assumed - we need those at ids 200-202.

vulkan-shaders-gen.cpp is +585 because my FIRST resolution of it was broken: a PowerShell -like test required a trailing space, never matched the real marker line, and silently truncated the file from 1266 lines to 891, dropping every TurboQuant shader registration. Caught by comparing every resolved file length against BOTH parents. Rewritten from theirs plus our five q2_0_g128 registrations, each edit asserting its expected occurrence count.

BUILD: GREEN. llama-server 86.9 MB, llama-cli 87.1 MB, llama-quantize 68.0 MB, cmake exit 0 AND the artifacts verified present on disk - an earlier run reported exit 0 from PowerShell while cmake never executed at all, because Out-File could not open a log another build still held.

Flags ON for the union, not the defaults: GGML_ARIFI_ROCMFPX_FORMATS=ON, GGML_ARIFI_TURBO_WEIGHT_QUANTS=ON, LLAMA_BUILD_MTMD=ON, Vulkan, CPU repack, embedded web UI.

NOT YET PROVEN: no model has been loaded on this binary. The formats are present in source and the build links, which is not the same as reading a file.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
